{%- comment -%}
  ============================================================================
  GOD SQUAD — password.liquid

  The layout behind the storefront password gate.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Shopify does not assign a layout by template type: a
  template either declares one or gets layout/theme.liquid. templates/password.json
  declared none, so the gate rendered inside the full storefront layout — the
  announcement bar, the header with its nav, search, account and cart controls,
  the cart drawer, cart.js and the whole footer — to a visitor who has not
  entered the password. Every destination behind the gate was listed on the gate.

  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT LOAD.
    - header-group and footer-group. Nothing behind the password may be named
      here; that is the entire point of the page.
    - cart.js, product.js, facets.js. There is no cart and no catalogue to act
      on, and a password page that ships the cart's JavaScript is shipping a
      surface it cannot render.
    - meta-social.liquid. A locked storefront should not publish a social card.
    - The section stylesheets. main-password.liquid requests its own.

  WHAT IT KEEPS. The design tokens and base.css, so the gate is recognisably the
  brand rather than an unstyled form; the fonts, for the same reason; the
  favicon; and content_for_header, which Shopify requires on every layout and
  which must never be modified or parsed.

  noindex. A store behind a password should not be indexed, and Shopify's own
  robots handling is not a substitute for saying so on the page.
  ============================================================================
{%- endcomment -%}
<!doctype html>
<html class="no-js" lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow">
    {% render 'css-variables', part: 'meta' %}
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">

    <title>{{ 'general.password_page.opening_soon' | t }} &ndash; {{ shop.name }}</title>

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
      <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 180, height: 180 }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The same font registration the main layout performs, and for the same
      reason: font_face returns a bare @font-face rule, not a style element, so
      the calls have to sit inside one {% style %}. Each font_modify is guarded
      because it returns nil when the family has no such variant.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign body_medium = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '500'
      assign body_semibold = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '600'
    -%}
    {% style %}
      {{ settings.type_display_font | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {{ settings.type_body_font | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- if body_medium %}
        {{ body_medium | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- endif %}
      {%- if body_semibold %}
        {{ body_semibold | font_face: font_display: 'swap' }}
      {%- endif %}
    {% endstyle %}

    {{ content_for_header }}

    {{ 'design-tokens.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {{ 'base.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {% render 'css-variables', part: 'style' %}
  </head>

  <body class="password-page">
    {%- comment -%}
      A main landmark and nothing else. There is no header to skip past, so
      there is no skip link either — a skip link whose target is the only
      content on the page is noise.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <main id="MainContent" role="main">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
